course: language
lesson: 47
title: The best ever and shocking: adverts and headlines
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Adverts and headlines use hyperbole for a job: to make you buy, click or read. «The BEST breakfast EVER!», «SHOCKING!!! You WON'T BELIEVE what happened next», «SNOW CHAOS! Britain grinds to a halt».
explain: Picture five centimetres of snow in southern England: a few trains late, a few schools shut, most people getting to work. Next morning a newspaper calls it «CHAOS». The snow was real; the size of the words was not.
explain: The wise reader looks past the big gold letters for the small print and the real facts: the label on the side of the box, the size of the toy, the actual temperature, who is paying for the praise.
explain: But not every number in an advert is made up: «8 out of 10 owners said their cats preferred it» is a real number — so the questions become: out of how many, and who asked them?
fact: «The best ever» is an opinion that sells; nobody can measure it, and «the best» does not mean healthy — the label on the side shows the sugar and what is really inside.
fact: Clickbait is a title or picture (a shocked face, red arrows, «you WON'T BELIEVE») made to look huge so that you click; what happened is usually ordinary, and you don't have to click.
fact: «The toy EVERYONE is talking about» means some people are talking about it, often because the advert wants them to; ask your friends and most will not have heard of it.
fact: Headlines make news sound bigger to sell papers and get clicks; the article underneath gives the real numbers.
fact: «UP TO 70% OFF» means the biggest discount in the shop, on only a few things; most things are only a little cheaper, so check the price tag of the thing you want.
fact: Close-up camera shots make toys look huge; the small print on the box («Truck length: 20 cm», «figures sold separately») tells the truth.
fact: «The BIGGEST update in HISTORY!!» is a company praising itself; wait for players' reviews and the real list of changes.
fact: «Brits SIZZLE as Britain hotter than the Sahara» might be true for one town against one place for one afternoon; the actual temperature and the weather service's advice are the facts.
fact: When someone earns money from what they praise (a discount code, «ad» or «sponsored»), expect big words; «it's giving main character» is slang for «it makes you feel special and cool».
fact: A real number can still be a small or unfair test: «8 out of 10 owners» may be only ten owners that the company chose.
fact: Weather warnings from the weather service (yellow, amber, red) are measured and calm; follow those, not a headline calling the third storm of the year «the storm of the century».
exercise: A cereal box says «The BEST breakfast EVER!». What is it? | An opinion meant to sell the cereal | A fact that scientists have checked | A promise that it is the healthiest | A prize the cereal won last year
exercise: Where on a cereal box can you find out how much sugar is in it? | The label on the side | The big gold letters | The picture on the front | The free toy inside
exercise: A thumbnail shows a shocked face, red arrows and «You WON'T BELIEVE what happened next 😱». What is this called? | Clickbait | A headline | A weather warning | A review
exercise: What usually happened in a «SHOCKING!!!» clickbait video? | Something quite ordinary | Something truly terrible | Something nobody has ever seen | Something against the law
exercise: An advert says «It's the toy EVERYONE is talking about!». What is the best way to check? | Ask your friends if they've heard of it | Believe it, because adverts must tell the truth | Watch the advert again to be sure | Buy it so you are not left out
exercise: Five centimetres of snow fall and some trains are late. A paper prints «SNOW CHAOS! Britain grinds to a halt». What really happened? | Some delays, but most people still got around | The whole country stopped for a week | Every train and bus in Britain was cancelled | Nothing at all, the snow was made up
exercise: Why do headlines often make news sound bigger? | To sell papers and get clicks | Because reporters cannot count | Because the law says they must | To make the news shorter to read
exercise: A sign says «SALE! UP TO 70% OFF!». What does «up to» mean? | That is the biggest discount, and most things have less | Everything in the whole shop has that much taken off | That is the smallest discount, and most things have more | The sale will only last until seventy people have come
exercise: A monster truck fills the screen in an advert. Where do you find its real size? | The small print on the box | The voice in the advert | The size of the TV screen | The word «MEGA» on the box
exercise: The box says «Truck length: 20 cm». About how big is that? | About as long as a pencil case | About as long as a real car | About as long as your little fingernail | About as long as a school bus
exercise: A game pop-up says «The BIGGEST update in HISTORY!!». What is the wise thing to do? | Wait for players' reviews and the real list of changes | Believe it, because games always know their own history | Tell everyone it is the biggest thing in history | Delete the game at once because it is lying
exercise: A tabloid says «HEATWAVE HELL! Britain hotter than the Sahara». It is 31 degrees. What is true? | It is hot for Britain | Britain is now a desert | It is as hot as hell | The Sahara is cold today
exercise: An influencer says a glitter slime «literally changed my life, it's giving main character» and shows a discount code. What should you remember? | She is probably being paid to praise it | A slime really can change your whole life | Discount codes mean the product is the best | «Main character» means the slime is in a film
exercise: What do «ad» or «sponsored» on a post tell you? | Someone is paying for the praise | The product has won a prize | The post is a true news story | The post was made by a school for its pupils
exercise: A cat food box says «8 out of 10 owners said their cats preferred it». What is a good question to ask? | Out of how many owners, and who asked them? | Why do cats not like the other two foods? | Which ten cats were the hungriest ones? | Is it hyperbole, like «a million cats»?
exercise: The news says «Storm of the century hits coast» — the third this year. What should you follow? | The official weather warnings | The biggest headline you can find | Nothing, headlines always shout | Your friend's guess about the storm
test: «bro this cereal says best breakfast ever so it's healthy right» What is right? | No, «best ever» is an opinion; read the label for sugar | Yes, the box would not say it unless it was healthy | Yes, gold letters are only allowed on healthy food | No, cereal can never be part of a good breakfast for anybody
test: Your little brother cries because the video says «You WON'T BELIEVE what happened next» and he is scared to find out. What can you tell him? | It's made to look huge so people click; it's probably ordinary | It is always something very scary, so never watch it | It means the video has been banned by the police | Nobody believes it, so the video is empty inside
test: «everyone's talking about this toy, I'm literally the only one without it» You ask your class and two people have heard of it. What happened? | The advert made it sound like everyone knew it | Everyone really has it but keeps very quiet about it at school | Your class must be the only one in the world | The two people made the advert themselves
test: «Mum, it says up to 70% off so my £20 game will be really cheap!» What is the problem? | «Up to» means only a few things have that much off; check the game's tag | Nothing, because every single thing in the shop is cheaper by exactly the same amount | The game will now cost £70 instead of the £20 it cost before | Sale signs are always against the law
test: «the advert truck was HUGE, why is this one tiny??» What is the best explanation? | The camera was close up; the box says 20 cm | The shop sent the wrong, smaller truck by mistake | The truck shrinks when you take it out | Big trucks are only sold in America
test: «it says Britain is hotter than the Sahara, are we gonna turn into desert lol» What do you say? | No, it's just hot for Britain; check the real temperature and advice | Yes, Britain will turn into a sandy desert by next summer, just like the Sahara | Yes, camels are already moving in | No, because Britain is always colder than everywhere
test: ★ «8 out of 10» is a real number, not hyperbole. Why can it still mislead you? | The company may have asked only a few owners that it chose | Real numbers can never mislead anyone, so it cannot mislead you at all | Eight is not really a number | Cats cannot be asked what they like
test: ★ What would happen if you panicked at every «storm of the century» headline? | You'd panic several times a year over ordinary storms | You would be safe from every storm for ever, because you'd always be ready | You would never see a storm again | Newspapers would stop writing headlines
test: ★ The opposite mistake: «headlines always shout, so I'll ignore the red weather warning». Why is this wrong? | A red warning is measured and calm, and it means real danger | Red warnings are only made up for the newspapers to sell more papers | Red warnings are a kind of headline hyperbole | The weather service always exaggerates storms
test: ★★★ A shop sign says «UP TO HALF PRICE!». A £20 game has a tag saying £15, and one rack has £10 items that were £20. Which statement is true? | The game is cheaper but not half price; only some things are | The game is half price, because the sign says so | Everything in the shop now costs £10 | The game costs more than before the sale
test: ★★★ «8 out of 10 owners said their cats preferred it». Which survey would you trust most? | 800 out of 1,000 owners asked by a group that doesn't sell cat food | 8 out of 10 owners chosen by the company that sells it | 4 out of 5 owners who work at the cat food factory | 1 out of 1 owner, the boss of the company
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the slogan «The BEST breakfast EVER!» printed upon a cereal packet? | It is an expression of opinion intended to promote sales | It is a measured fact verified by scientific inquiry | It is a guarantee of the nutritional quality of the product | It is an official award conferred upon the manufacturer
exam: A retail notice proclaims «UP TO 70% OFF». Select the response consistent with the established meaning of the words «up to». | The figure stated is the greatest reduction, applicable to a limited number of items | The figure stated is applied uniformly to every article in the shop | The figure stated is the smallest reduction available | The figure stated refers to the number of days remaining
exam: Identify the principal reason for which newspaper headlines frequently represent events as larger than they were. | To attract readers and thereby sell papers and obtain clicks | To comply with the regulations governing the press | To compensate for errors in measurement | To shorten the article that follows
exam: It is asserted that «8 out of 10 owners» preferred a certain cat food. Ascertain which question is most pertinent in evaluating the claim. | How many owners were consulted, and by whom | Whether eight is a greater number than ten | Whether the remaining owners possess cats | Whether the claim is printed in capital letters
